<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yeavering</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ad Gefrin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yeavering, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, is the site of Ad Gefrin, a royal residence of the 7th-century kings of Bernicia and Northumbria below the hillfort of Yeavering Bell. Excavations from 1953 revealed great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> halls and a wedge-shaped wooden grandstand for assemblies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> records that the missionary Paulinus baptised people in the River Glen there for 36 days around 627.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yeavering.jpg" alt="Yeavering" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Webb · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yeavering_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1381782.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yeavering lies on the River Glen at the western end of Glendale, where the foothills of the Cheviot Hills give way to the fertile Tweed Valley. Its most prominent feature is the twin-peaked Yeavering Bell, 353 metres high; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement stood to its north on a terrace known as the whaleback. The name, first recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> as ad gefrin, comes from Brittonic words meaning hill of the goats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mesolithic tools, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> pits and cremation burials show early activity in the valley. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the largest hillfort in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> was built on Yeavering Bell, with dry stone walls around both peaks and more than a hundred roundhouses; later written sources name the people of the area as the Votadini. Romano-British finds include two late Roman coins and shards of Samian ware.</p><p>The archaeologist Brian Hope-Taylor, who excavated from 1953 to 1957 and again from 1960 to 1962 after aerial photographs had shown crop marks, argued that the kings of Bernicia ruled from two seats, Bamburgh on the coast and Yeavering among the native Britons inland. He found a double-palisaded Great Enclosure, Great Halls of up to about 300 square metres, and the grave of an adult buried with a goat's skull and a bronze-bound pole, perhaps a ceremonial staff. Later scholars, among them Christopher Scull and Tim Gates, have questioned his dating and see the site as an ordinary Anglian farming settlement of the 6th century, later elaborated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says it was replaced by Maelmin, a site known from crop marks at Milfield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yeavering is a hamlet in the civil parish of Kirknewton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>. The site of Ad Gefrin was bought in 2000 by the archaeologist Roger Miket and placed in the care of the Gefrin Trust, which first met in 2004 and includes representatives of local government, English Heritage and the academic world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yeavering” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal centres such as Bamburgh, <b>Yeavering</b>, where excavations revealed a great timber hall and assembly structure, and later York formed the hubs of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Oswald brought Aidan from Iona, who founded Lindisfarne in 635, and royal sites such as <b>Yeavering</b> show grand halls.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bernicia">Kingdom of Bernicia</a></li></ul></section>
